WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.103
Imagine a debugger that stops at a breakpoint. A
tightly coupled implementation calls the

00:00:05.103 --> 00:00:09.919
editor to highlight a line, calls the variables
pane to refresh, and calls a history

00:00:09.919 --> 00:00:14.563
component to record the stop. Adding another
view requires changing the debugger.

00:00:14.563 --> 00:00:19.713
With implicit invocation, the debugger announces
that execution stopped and registered

00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:24.982
handlers decide how to react. The event carries
enough context, such as the location and

00:00:24.982 --> 00:00:30.371
execution identifier, for each interested
component. The producer now depends on the event

00:00:30.371 --> 00:00:33.305
contract rather than every view's
implementation.

00:00:33.305 --> 00:00:37.821
Draw one event leaving the debugger and several
reactions branching from it. This makes

00:00:37.821 --> 00:00:42.182
extension easier while preserving an explicit
description of what happened and which

00:00:42.182 --> 00:00:43.584
information accompanies it.

00:00:43.805 --> 00:00:48.783
The same event abstraction can run synchronously
inside one process. Calling an event

00:00:48.783 --> 00:00:53.937
dispatcher may invoke every handler before
returning, so a slow handler still delays the

00:00:53.937 --> 00:00:58.856
producer. A distributed broker often introduces
asynchronous delivery, but that is a

00:00:58.856 --> 00:01:01.902
connector choice rather than a definition of
events.

00:01:01.902 --> 00:01:06.888
Ask what happens when a handler fails, whether
other handlers still execute, and whether

00:01:06.888 --> 00:01:12.101
ordering is guaranteed. Without those answers,
the simple fan-out diagram hides control-flow

00:01:12.101 --> 00:01:17.200
obligations. A debugger extension that throws an
exception should not accidentally prevent

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.657
the editor from updating unless that behavior is
intentional.

00:01:20.902 --> 00:01:25.999
Trace a failed handler through the dispatch
semantics and document the result; modularity

00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:30.294
requires a predictable interaction contract as
well as separate components.

00:01:30.402 --> 00:01:35.272
When a course seat becomes available, a
registration system can emit one event. A

00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:40.504
notification subscriber alerts waiting students,
an analytics subscriber updates demand

00:01:40.504 --> 00:01:45.855
statistics, and an audit subscriber records the
change. If all three need the event, they

00:01:45.855 --> 00:01:49.824
require independent subscriptions or equivalent
fan-out semantics.

00:01:49.902 --> 00:01:55.123
Three competing workers sharing one work queue
would instead divide the messages, so some

00:01:55.123 --> 00:02:00.286
reactions would never happen. Within the
notification subscription, multiple workers can

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:04.745
share the delivery workload. Draw these two
levels separately: copies across

00:02:04.745 --> 00:02:08.030
responsibilities, competition within one
responsibility.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.838
This distinction explains why the words producer
and consumer do not by themselves tell you

00:02:12.838 --> 00:02:17.328
whether every service sees every event or
whether each event goes to just one worker.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:22.665
Suppose the seat event contains only a course
code. A notification consumer may need to

00:02:22.665 --> 00:02:28.036
query another service for the section and
availability version before it can act.
Including

00:02:28.036 --> 00:02:33.171
more facts reduces that dependency but can
increase payload size, privacy exposure, and

00:02:33.171 --> 00:02:34.469
schema evolution work.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:39.687
There is no contract-free event bus: every
consumer relies on the meaning of fields and on

00:02:39.687 --> 00:02:44.844
rules for missing or changed fields. Use
identifiers and versions to distinguish the
event

00:02:44.844 --> 00:02:50.001
from the current entity state. If a requester
requires a specific reply before proceeding,

00:02:50.001 --> 00:02:53.438
represent that obligation with correlation and a
reply path.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:58.667
Hiding a required response inside an event does
not remove the dependency; it only makes

00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:01.410
that dependency harder to observe and diagnose.
